  "image_name": "2a55f0c060c8a9066b8c339628ef133e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how microRNAs can be packaged into exosomes, travel through the bloodstream, and regulate gene expression in another cell, while also highlighting points for therapeutic intervention.\n\nIn the donor cell, precursor miRNAs are loaded into multivesicular bodies. These compartments fuse with the plasma membrane and release exosomes containing miRNA into the extracellular space. A labeled inhibitor, GW4869, is shown blocking this release step, indicating suppression of exosome secretion. The exosomes then circulate in the bloodstream among red and white blood cells and are taken up by a recipient cell through endocytosis.\n\nOnce inside the recipient cell, the exosomal miRNA is released and processed by Dicer into mature miRNA. This mature miRNA associates with RISC and binds a target mRNA, shown near the 3′ UTR, where it appears to interfere with translation or promote silencing. The diagram also overlays therapeutic strategies: miRNA mimics can supplement or restore miRNA activity, whereas anti-miR approaches such as LNA, antagomirs, or miRNA sponges can inhibit it.\n\nOverall, the image presents exosome-mediated miRNA transfer as a pathway for intercellular communication and a framework for drug targeting.",
